(window.TPR_CH = window.TPR_CH || []).push(
{"id":"c-u-11","n":1411,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🌙","title":"4 a.m.: he wants to go home after a hanging attempt","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 04:00, on call","where":"Adult acute ward, interview room by the nursing office","text":"Gareth Lloyd, 45, was admitted informally last night after trying to hang himself in his loft. He wrote a note and told no one; the rope snapped, and neck pain made him ring for an ambulance. Liaison saw him in A&E after medical clearance: his wife recently left with their two young children, and he feels hopeless. Within hours of arriving he is in his coat asking to discharge himself. The nurse in charge bleeps you."},"task":"Assess Gareth's risk to himself and others in about ten minutes, decide whether he can safely leave tonight, and explain the plan and his rights to him.","first60":["Ask the nurse in charge for two minutes: what has he said, has he tried the door, and is a nurse with him now?","Skim the liaison assessment and the A&E notes: method, note, how he was found, neck injury findings and medical clearance.","Check his status on the system: informal, no previous detentions, no forensic history, no previous self-harm recorded.","Agree with the nurse in charge: if he heads for the exit before you finish, she can use the nurse's holding power, Section 5(4).","Know who to ring afterwards: the on-call higher trainee or consultant, before any holding power is used."],"ask":["'What's made you decide you need to go right now, at four in the morning?'","'Talk me through the night it happened. How long had you been planning it?'","'When the rope gave way, how did you feel? And how do you feel about it now?'","'Are you still having thoughts about ending your life? Have you thought how?'","'If you went home now, what would you do first?'","'If the children didn't want to come with you, what then?'","'Have you had any thoughts of harming anyone else, including your wife or the children?'","'Is anyone threatening you or taking advantage of you at the moment?'"],"recognise":["Hanging with a note, timed to avoid discovery, survived only because the rope failed: this is a near-lethal attempt with high intent.","No regret, uncertain about trying again, and a 'last chance' plan that may fail tonight: risk is current, not historical.","Two months of low mood, tearfulness, no enjoyment, poor concentration and pessimism: a depressive episode, untreated so far.","Male, 45, newly separated, living alone, no treatment yet: the demographic risk adds to the clinical risk.","Wanting to collect his children while hopeless and suicidal is a possible risk to them; ask directly, even if he denies it.","Leaving at 4 a.m. with no transport, no senior review and no plan shows he has not weighed up the consequences."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain that you think it is not safe for him to leave tonight, ask him to stay informally until the consultant sees him in the morning, start continuous observation, and tell him honestly that if he tries to leave, the team would use a holding power to arrange a Mental Health Act assessment.","r":"Right: proportionate, honest and least restrictive. He keeps his informal status if he agrees, but the near-lethal risk is covered.","best":true},{"c":"Let him go because he is informal and has capacity, with the crisis team number.","r":"Unsafe: a near-lethal attempt less than a day ago, ongoing intent and a plan that may fail tonight. Crisis team follow-up is not enough at this level of risk.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him he is now 'sectioned' to stop the argument.","r":"Wrong and unlawful: no holding power is in place, and threatening one to gain compliance breaches the Code of Practice. Be honest about what would happen if he tried to leave.","best":false}],"law":["Informal patients may leave unless a holding power is used. Section 5(4) lets a registered mental health or learning disability nurse hold him for up to 6 hours (Form H2).","Section 5(2) lets the doctor or approved clinician in charge of his treatment, or one nominated deputy (usually the on-call doctor at night), hold him for up to 72 hours (Form H1, delivered to the hospital managers).","Section 5(2) is only for an informal inpatient who indicates he wants to leave; it is a pause for a full assessment by an AMHP and two doctors, not a treatment order.","Code of Practice: never threaten detention to gain compliance, but be open about what would happen if he tried to leave.","Under a holding power he can still refuse treatment; the consent rules of Part 4 do not apply to Section 5."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Gareth, I can see you badly want to be home, and I'm not dismissing that. But a day ago you nearly died, and you've told me you might try again. I don't think it's safe for you to leave tonight."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I'd like you to stay as you are, voluntarily, until the consultant sees you this morning. I'll be honest with you: if you tried to leave, we'd use a short legal holding power so a full assessment can happen. I'd much rather we agree."},{"to":"On-call consultant","s":"Gareth Lloyd, 45, informal, admitted after a near-lethal hanging with a note. Wants to leave to collect his children; no regret, can't rule out another attempt. Agreed to stay on continuous observation. If he tries to leave I'll use 5(2). Please review at nine."}],"write":"04:15 On-call review, adult ward. Gareth Lloyd, 45M, informal. Asked to self-discharge.\nAttempt (D0): hanging from loft beam, note left for children, timed to avoid discovery; rope snapped. No regret. Unsure if he would try again.\nCurrent: wants to go home to bring children back from wife; 'last chance'; if they refuse, 'no point'. Denies intent to harm wife or children. Denies risk from others. No previous self-harm, no forensic history, no alcohol or drugs.\nMental state: low, tearful, hopeless; 2 months low mood, anhedonia, poor concentration, pessimism. No psychotic symptoms. Neck: bruising, no hoarseness, stridor or difficulty swallowing.\nCapacity to decide on leaving: unable to weigh risks (no transport, no plan, no regard for consequences).\nRisk: HIGH to self; possible risk to children, low-certainty.\nPlan: agreed to remain informally; continuous observation within eyesight; belongings searched with consent, ligature items removed; nurse in charge aware 5(4) if he tries to leave; I will use 5(2) if needed (nominated deputy). Discussed with Dr Patel, on-call consultant. Consultant review 09:00, consider MHA assessment. Medics to review if any voice change or swallowing difficulty.\nDr Chinonso, CT1, on call","make":["Sit down, slow the pace, and name his wish to leave before you argue with it.","Get the attempt in detail: planning, note, timing, method, how he survived, and how he feels about surviving.","Ask about now: thoughts, plan, means at home, and what he would do in the first hour after leaving.","Ask directly about risk to others and from others, especially the children.","State your decision plainly, with your reasons and his rights, then the observation level.","Ring a senior and write it up before you leave the ward."],"next":"Gareth stays informally on continuous observation and sleeps from 5 a.m. At 9 a.m. the consultant finds him still hopeless and wanting to leave, and arranges a Mental Health Act assessment; he is detained under Section 2. A week into sertraline and daily talks with his named nurse, he agrees to a supervised call with his children.","marks":["Acknowledged his wish to leave and agreed to talk first.","Took a detailed history of the attempt: planning, note, method, lethality and regret.","Assessed current intent, plans and means, and what he would do on leaving.","Screened for depression and psychosis, and asked about risk to and from others.","Explained a clear decision with reasons, his rights and the holding powers honestly.","Set observation level, ward safety and senior review, and documented."],"pitfalls":["Letting him leave because he is 'informal and has capacity' without weighing the attempt.","Threatening to 'section' him to win the argument.","Forgetting to ask about the children and risk to others.","Not checking the neck for hoarseness or swallowing problems after a hanging.","Using 5(2) without telling a senior, or not completing Form H1."],"refs":["1. Mental Health Act 1983, Section 5, and the Code of Practice (2015), chapter 18 (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG225 (self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence, 2022; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022), 2022; checked Nov 2026)","4. NCISH annual report: suicide after recent discharge and among informal inpatients (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: at night the on-call doctor is usually the nominated deputy for Section 5(2). Know your trust's list, keep Form H1 to hand, and ring your senior before you use it. NICE NG225 warns against using risk scales to decide who leaves.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-13","n":1413,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"❤️","title":"Chest pain on the ward: a ten-minute heart check","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 15:10, ward doctor","where":"Acute adult ward, patient's bedroom","text":"Brenda Oyelaran, 63, was admitted a few days ago with repeated bouts of anxiety in which she gets short of breath. This afternoon she told the nurses she has pain in her chest. The healthcare assistant is taking her observations as you walk to her room. The ECG machine is on the corridor."},"task":"In about ten minutes, triage Brenda's chest pain, get an ECG done, carry out a full cardiovascular examination while keeping her informed, and escalate to the right medical team.","first60":["Ask the healthcare assistant to bring the ECG machine now: a 12-lead ECG should be done within ten minutes of chest pain.","Glance at the NEWS2 chart as it is filled in: pulse, BP, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, temperature, consciousness.","Check the drug chart: anything that lengthens QT, any recent dose changes, aspirin allergy, and whether GTN is prescribed.","Know where the emergency trolley, oxygen and defibrillator are, and the number for the medical emergency team or 999.","Wash your hands and bring a chaperone into the room."],"ask":["'Is the pain there right now? Out of ten, how bad?'","'When did it start, and what were you doing?'","'Where is it, and does it spread to your arm, neck, jaw or back?'","'What does it feel like: tight, heavy, sharp, burning?'","'Any sweating, sickness, palpitations or feeling faint with it?'","'Does it change when you breathe in, move or press on your chest?'","'Any heart problems, high blood pressure, diabetes, smoking or family heart disease?'","'Any calf pain, recent surgery or long journeys?'"],"recognise":["A 63-year-old with chest pain is a cardiac patient until proved otherwise, even if she is admitted with panic.","Panic and angina share breathlessness, tight chest and fear; only an ECG, observations and examination separate them at the bedside.","A normal ECG does not rule out acute coronary syndrome: troponin and senior medical review decide that.","Unequal blood pressure in the two arms with tearing pain suggests aortic dissection: an emergency.","Pleuritic pain, low oxygen saturation, fast heart rate or a swollen calf suggest pulmonary embolism, more likely in inactive inpatients.","Psychotropics matter: antipsychotics can lengthen QT; clozapine can cause myocarditis in the first weeks."],"decide":[{"c":"Get an ECG and NEWS2 at once, take a quick pain history, examine fully, and ring the medical on-call for review and a high-sensitivity troponin; call 999 or the emergency team if pain persists or the ECG changes.","r":"Right: fast, structured and safe. Her anxiety diagnosis does not change the cardiac work-up.","best":true},{"c":"Give her PRN lorazepam for the panic and review her later.","r":"Unsafe: it may mask a heart attack and delays the ECG. Treat chest pain as cardiac until shown otherwise.","best":false},{"c":"Send her to A&E without examining her or doing an ECG.","r":"Not ideal: the ECG takes minutes and decides how urgent the transfer is. Examine and record first unless she is collapsing.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity to consent to examination; explain each step and offer a chaperone, recording who was present.","If she refuses examination or transfer, assess capacity for that decision; treatment for a physical problem falls under the Mental Capacity Act, not the Mental Health Act.","Trust physical health policy: NEWS2 of 5 or more, or 3 in a single parameter, needs urgent medical review."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Brenda, chest pain always gets checked properly here, whatever else is going on. I'll do a heart tracing and examine you, and I'll tell you what I find as I go."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Your heart tracing and examination look reassuring. Because of your age and your dad's history, I'd still like a blood test for your heart, so I'm asking the medical doctor to see you."},{"to":"Medical on-call","s":"Brenda Oyelaran, 63, psychiatric inpatient admitted with panic. Central tight chest pain for 40 minutes, now 2 out of 10. Hypertensive, ex-smoker, father had an MI at 60. NEWS2 2, ECG sinus 96, no ST changes, QTc 440. Examination normal. Please review and advise on troponin."}],"write":"15:25 Ward review: chest pain. Chaperone: HCA Joy.\nHx: central tight chest pain started 14:30 during panic attack, max 6/10, now 2/10. No radiation, sweating, nausea or syncope. Not pleuritic or positional. No calf pain, recent surgery or travel. PMH: HTN (amlodipine 5 mg). Ex-smoker 20 pack-years. FH: father MI at 60. Meds: sertraline 50 mg. No aspirin allergy.\nObs: HR 96 reg, BP 148/88 R and 146/86 L, RR 18, SpO2 97% air, T 36.8, alert. NEWS2 2.\nECG: sinus rhythm 96, normal axis, no ST or T changes, QTc 440 ms.\nO/E: anxious, not sweaty. Hands warm, CRT under 2 s, no clubbing or stigmata. No radio-radial delay or collapsing pulse. No pallor. JVP not raised. Carotids normal. Apex 5th ICS MCL, no heaves or thrills. HS I+II+0. Chest clear. No sacral or ankle oedema; calves soft, non-tender.\nImp: chest pain during panic attack; ACS not excluded on history and risk factors.\nPlan: discussed with medical SHO on call (Dr Ahmed), reviewing within 30 min; high-sensitivity troponin; FBC, U&E, lipids, HbA1c. Repeat ECG if pain recurs. 999 / emergency team if pain persists over 15 min, ECG changes or NEWS2 rises. Nurses aware.\nDr Chinonso, CT1","make":["Ask for the ECG before you do anything else; examine while it is being set up.","Get the five key pain facts in one minute: onset, site, character, radiation, associated symptoms.","Examine in a fixed order, hands to heart to lungs to legs, talking to her as you go.","Check blood pressure in both arms and the calves: dissection and PE hide here.","Don't let 'she's anxious' close your mind: escalate with a clear SBAR call."],"next":"The medical SHO reviews Brenda within half an hour. Her two high-sensitivity troponins are normal, and the repeat ECG is unchanged. Cardiology advise an outpatient assessment. Once the medics are satisfied, the ward psychologist starts CBT for panic, and Brenda says she feels 'taken seriously for the first time'.","marks":["Prioritised an ECG and observations and asked about current pain.","Took a focused cardiac pain history and risk factors.","Performed hand hygiene, consent, chaperone, positioning and dignified exposure.","Examined hands, pulse, both-arm BP, face, neck, praecordium, lungs and legs in order.","Explained each step and reassured her during the examination.","Summarised findings and escalated to the medical team with a clear safety net."],"pitfalls":["Putting the pain down to panic before an ECG.","Giving a benzodiazepine first and examining later.","Forgetting blood pressure in both arms or the calves.","Examining in silence, or commenting on findings to the nurse rather than the patient.","Treating a normal ECG as the all-clear."],"refs":["1. NICE CG95 (recent-onset chest pain of suspected cardiac origin, updated 2016; checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Physicians: NEWS2 (2017; checked Nov 2026)","3. Resuscitation Council UK: ABCDE approach (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: aspirin and glyceryl trinitrate in acute coronary syndrome (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: on UK psychiatric wards you are the first doctor for physical emergencies. Know whether your unit calls an internal emergency team or 999, and use SBAR when you phone the medical on-call.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-14","n":1414,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🦋","title":"Swollen neck on lithium: short history and thyroid examination","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:30, ward doctor","where":"Acute adult ward, clinic room","text":"Kerry-Ann Mensah, 39, has bipolar affective disorder and was admitted during a depressive episode. She has taken lithium for several years, and it was restarted at her usual dose on admission. Over the past few weeks she has noticed her neck looking fuller and has told the nurses. The nurse in charge asks you to see her before the afternoon ward round."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a brief medical history about Kerry-Ann's neck swelling, carry out a thyroid examination, and tell her what you think it is and what happens next.","first60":["Check the results system: last TSH and free T4, lithium level and date, eGFR, calcium.","Check the drug chart: lithium dose and brand (prescribe by brand), and any NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors or diuretics that raise levels.","Bring a glass of water, a tendon hammer and a stethoscope; she will need to sit on a chair you can stand behind.","Ask a female member of staff to chaperone, and wash your hands."],"ask":["'When did you first notice the swelling, and has it grown?'","'Is it painful? Any trouble swallowing, breathing, or changes in your voice?'","'How's your energy? Have you put on weight without eating more?'","'Do you feel the cold more than other people?'","'Any change in your bowels, skin, hair or periods? Any change in your sex drive?'","'Any feeling hot, shaky, sweaty, or a racing heart?'","'When was your last lithium blood test and thyroid test?'","'Anyone in your family with thyroid problems?'"],"recognise":["Lithium commonly causes an underactive thyroid and a goitre, more often in women and in the first years of treatment.","Tiredness, weight gain on the same appetite, cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin, coarse hair and low libido make a clear hypothyroid pattern.","Hypothyroidism can deepen depression and cause rapid cycling in bipolar disorder: it may be part of why she was admitted.","A smooth, symmetrical, painless swelling that rises on swallowing is a diffuse goitre; a hard, fixed or single lump needs an urgent ultrasound.","A pulse of 58 and slow-relaxing ankle reflexes are bedside signs of hypothyroidism.","Lithium also raises calcium (hyperparathyroidism) and can impair the kidneys: check calcium and eGFR at the same time."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a focused thyroid history, examine fully, send TSH, free T4, antibodies, calcium, U&Es and a 12-hour lithium level, keep lithium going, and discuss levothyroxine with the consultant once results are back.","r":"Right: confirms lithium-related hypothyroidism, checks for its partners (calcium, kidneys) and protects her mood stability.","best":true},{"c":"Stop lithium today because it has damaged her thyroid.","r":"Wrong: stopping suddenly risks rebound mania. Hypothyroidism is treated with levothyroxine while lithium usually continues.","best":false},{"c":"Reassure her it's just weight gain from the ward food.","r":"Unsafe: misses a treatable cause of her tiredness and low mood, and a swelling that may need imaging.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity to consent to examination; explain first and offer a chaperone, recording who was present.","Any change to lithium or a new levothyroxine prescription is discussed with her and her consultant, and the GP is told for shared care.","Prescribe lithium by brand; switching brands changes the level."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Kerry-Ann, the tiredness, feeling cold and the weight gain, together with this smooth swelling, make me think your thyroid has become a bit underactive. Lithium can do that, and it's very treatable."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Please don't stop your lithium. If the blood test confirms it, a small daily thyroid tablet usually puts things right, and you can stay on lithium."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Kerry-Ann Mensah: one month of painless diffuse neck swelling with classic hypothyroid symptoms. Smooth symmetrical goitre, no nodes, pulse 58, slow-relaxing ankle reflexes. I've sent TFTs, antibodies, calcium, U&Es and a 12-hour lithium level. Can we discuss levothyroxine on today's round?"}],"write":"11:45 Ward review: neck swelling. Chaperone: SN Ade.\nHx: noticed neck fuller 1 month, gradually; painless. No dysphagia, stridor or voice change. Tired, weight gain on same appetite, cold intolerant, constipated (BO 4 days ago), dry skin, coarse hair, reduced libido, periods heavier. No heat intolerance, palpitations or sweating. Lithium for 6 years; last TFTs over a year ago. FH: mother hypothyroid.\nO/E: comfortable; dry skin. Hands cool, no sweating, onycholysis or acropachy. Pulse 58 regular. Fine tremor (consistent with lithium). No exophthalmos, lid lag or ophthalmoplegia. Neck: smooth, symmetrical, non-tender diffuse swelling, rises on swallowing, not with tongue protrusion. No nodules. No cervical lymphadenopathy. Trachea central. No retrosternal dullness. No bruit. Ankle reflexes slow-relaxing. No proximal weakness. No pretibial myxoedema or oedema.\nImp: likely lithium-associated hypothyroidism with diffuse goitre.\nPlan: TSH, fT4, TPO antibodies, U&E/eGFR, adjusted calcium, lithium level 12 h post-dose tomorrow 08:00. Continue lithium. Discuss levothyroxine on ward round. USS thyroid if nodular or enlarging. Told to report hoarse voice, swallowing or breathing difficulty. GP to be updated.\nDr Chinonso, CT1","make":["Ask the full hypothyroid sweep in one go: energy, weight, cold, bowels, skin and hair, periods, libido, mood.","Ask the three red flags for any neck lump: swallowing, breathing, voice.","Examine in a fixed order: general, hands and pulse, eyes, neck from the front with a swallow, neck from behind, then reflexes and legs.","Comment to her, not to the nurse, and phrase appearance findings kindly.","Tell her clearly: keep taking lithium."],"next":"Kerry-Ann's TSH comes back at 14 mU/L with a low free T4 and positive antibodies; her calcium and 12-hour lithium level (0.7 mmol/L) are normal. The consultant starts levothyroxine and keeps lithium unchanged. Over the following weeks her energy and mood lift, and the GP takes over thyroid monitoring after discharge.","marks":["Introduced self, gained consent and offered a chaperone.","Asked about the swelling and the red flags of swallowing, breathing and voice.","Covered hypothyroid symptoms, including periods and libido, and lithium history.","Examined hands, pulse, eyes and the neck from front and behind with swallow.","Checked nodes, trachea, bruit, reflexes and proximal power.","Explained likely lithium-related hypothyroidism and the plan, advising her to continue lithium."],"pitfalls":["Skipping the history and going straight to the neck.","Forgetting the swallow test or palpating only from the front.","Telling her to stop lithium.","Forgetting calcium and kidney function alongside the thyroid tests.","Commenting on her weight or skin in a way that embarrasses her."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185 (bipolar disorder: assessment and management, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG145 (thyroid disease: assessment and management, 2019; checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: lithium carbonate, monitoring and interactions; levothyroxine (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS England: lithium patient information pack and record book (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK lithium is prescribed by brand and patients carry a purple lithium record book. Use it to check the last level, thyroid and kidney results before you examine.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-16","n":1416,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🫁","title":"Feverish and breathless three weeks into olanzapine: the night-time chest check","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 02:10, night on call","where":"Women's acute adult psychiatry ward, standalone mental health unit","text":"The night nurse bleeps you about Sinead Fairweather, 39, detained under Section 2 after a relapse of psychosis. She started olanzapine three weeks ago and was settling. For four days she has been coughing up green phlegm; tonight she is breathless and her temperature is 39.6°C. Her latest observations: respiratory rate 24, SpO2 95% on air, pulse 106, BP 112/70, alert. The nearest acute hospital is three miles away."},"task":"Assess her chest at the bedside in about ten minutes: focused questions, a systematic respiratory check front and back, a NEWS2 score, and a clear, safe plan for the night.","first60":["Ask the nurse for a fresh full set of observations and a NEWS2 score before you walk in.","Bring a female nurse as chaperone, a stethoscope, gloves and a sputum pot; know where the oxygen and pulse oximeter are.","Check the drug chart: olanzapine dose and start date, any other sedatives, allergies, and whether she smokes.","Check her legal status: on Section 2 she needs Section 17 leave, with escort, to go to the acute hospital.","Wash your hands and draw the curtains before she undresses."],"ask":["'When did the cough start, and what colour is the phlegm?'","'Does it hurt when you breathe in deeply?'","'Any blood in the phlegm, or pain or swelling in your calves?'","'Have you ever had asthma or any other chest problem? Do you smoke?'","'Have you choked or coughed while eating or drinking since the new tablet?'","'Do your muscles feel stiff, or do you feel muddled?'","'Can you take a deep breath in for me, and out?'"],"recognise":["Four days of productive cough, fever 39.6°C and breathlessness: a lower respiratory tract infection until proven otherwise.","NEWS2 of 6 (RR 24 = 2, SpO2 95% = 1, pulse 106 = 1, temp 39.6 = 2) means an urgent response: senior or medical review within the hour.","Dull right base, bronchial breathing, coarse crackles and louder vocal resonance there point to right lower lobe consolidation.","Antipsychotics raise pneumonia risk, partly through sedation and swallowing problems; ask about choking on food or drink.","Fever on a new antipsychotic: check for rigidity, sweating and confusion so you do not miss neuroleptic malignant syndrome.","Breathless after weeks on a ward and a new antipsychotic: check the calves, because both raise clot risk."],"decide":[{"c":"Finish the chest check, call the acute medical registrar, arrange urgent transfer with Section 17 leave and escort, send bloods including cultures and CK, and repeat NEWS2 every 30 minutes until she goes.","r":"Right: a NEWS2 of 6 with likely pneumonia needs an X-ray, blood tests and antibiotics within the hour, which a standalone mental health unit cannot safely provide overnight.","best":true},{"c":"Start oral amoxicillin on the ward and review in the morning.","r":"Wrong: she has signs of sepsis, and nobody has confirmed the diagnosis or checked her bloods and lactate. Waiting until morning risks rapid deterioration.","best":false},{"c":"Stop the olanzapine tonight because it caused the pneumonia.","r":"Unwise: stopping abruptly risks relapse and does not treat the infection. Flag the link to her consultant and the medical team, and review the dose tomorrow.","best":false}],"law":["On Section 2, leaving the unit for the acute hospital needs Section 17 leave from the responsible clinician; at night, the on-call consultant can grant it. Record it and send an escort.","Section 2 authorises treatment for mental disorder only. Treating her pneumonia needs her consent, or a best-interests decision under the Mental Capacity Act if she lacks capacity.","Explain each step and gain consent before you touch her; she can stop you at any time.","Offer a chaperone for any intimate check and record the chaperone's name."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Sinead, I think you have a chest infection in the bottom of your right lung. You need an X-ray and stronger treatment tonight, so we're sending you to the main hospital with a nurse."},{"to":"Acute medical registrar","s":"39-year-old woman on Section 2, three weeks on olanzapine. Four days' productive cough, temp 39.6, RR 24, sats 95% on air, pulse 106. NEWS2 6. Right basal consolidation signs. I'd like her seen tonight."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"Please repeat her observations every 30 minutes. If her NEWS2 reaches 7, sats fall below 94% or she becomes drowsy, call 999 and bleep me straight away."}],"write":"On-call psychiatry, 02:20. Asked by night nurse re fever and SOB. Chaperone: SN Okafor. Consent given.\nHx: 4/7 cough, green sputum; SOB since this evening. No pleuritic pain, haemoptysis or calf pain. No asthma/COPD. Smokes 10/day. Occasional coughing when drinking since olanzapine. No stiffness.\nObs: RR 24, SpO2 95% RA, HR 106 reg, BP 112/70, T 39.6, Alert. NEWS2 6.\nO/E: speaking in sentences, mild accessory muscle use, sputum pot green. No clubbing, cyanosis or flap. Trachea central, no nodes, JVP not raised. No rigidity. Reduced expansion R base, dull percussion, bronchial breathing, coarse crackles and increased vocal resonance R lower zone. L clear. Calves soft, non-tender; no ankle oedema.\nImp: R lower lobe pneumonia with sepsis risk. NMS and PE considered: unlikely.\nPlan: d/w medical registrar, accepted to AMU. Section 17 leave granted by on-call consultant Dr Reid; RMN escort. Bloods: FBC, U&E, CRP, LFT, CK, lactate (VBG), blood cultures x2 before antibiotics; sputum MC&S. Obs every 30 min; call 999 if NEWS2 ≥7 or SpO2 <94%. Olanzapine: continue, flag to RC for review in morning; ask SALT re swallow.\nDr Chinonso, CT1 on call","make":["Score NEWS2 out loud before you touch her: it sets the speed of everything after.","Talk to her, not past her: 'That cough sounds rough, are you bringing anything up?'","Compare left and right at each level, front then back; the back is where the bases are.","Look for rigidity and calf swelling: two quick checks that catch the dangerous alternatives.","Name the plan and the legal step: medical transfer, Section 17 leave, escort, and who you have told."],"next":"The medical registrar sees Sinead at 03:30. Her X-ray shows right lower lobe consolidation and she starts antibiotics. CK is normal. She returns to the ward four days later. Her consultant keeps olanzapine at the same dose, and a speech and language therapist assesses her swallow.","marks":["Hand hygiene, identity, consent and a female chaperone.","Stated the NEWS2 score and what it means.","Focused history: onset, sputum, pleuritic pain, haemoptysis, calves, smoking, swallowing.","Systematic check: inspection, hands, neck, then expansion, percussion, auscultation and vocal resonance front and back.","Checked for rigidity and DVT.","Explained the likely pneumonia and arranged urgent medical transfer with Section 17 leave and a safety net."],"pitfalls":["Skipping the back of the chest, where lower lobe signs are found.","Treating on the ward overnight without bloods, cultures, an X-ray or a lactate.","Forgetting she is detained: no Section 17 leave, no escort.","Missing neuroleptic malignant syndrome or a clot in a febrile, breathless patient on a new antipsychotic.","Commenting on findings to the nurse but saying nothing to the patient."],"refs":["1. Royal College of Physicians: National Early Warning Score (NEWS) 2 (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG51: suspected sepsis, recognition and early management (updated 2024; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG138: pneumonia (community-acquired), antimicrobial prescribing (checked Nov 2026)","4. MHA 1983 Code of Practice, chapter 27: Section 17 leave (checked Nov 2026)","5. BNF: olanzapine (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: many UK psychiatric units stand alone, with no X-ray, no lab on site and no IV antibiotics at night. Your job is to recognise the sick patient fast, score NEWS2, start the right tests and get her to the medical team.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-17","n":1417,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🩺","title":"Clozapine and twelve days without a bowel movement: the urgent abdominal check","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 11:40, ward round just finished","where":"Adult acute inpatient ward, mental health unit","text":"The nurse in charge stops you: Lewis Marangoni, 45, an informal patient on clozapine 400 mg a day, due for discharge next week, has just admitted he has not opened his bowels for 12 days. He kept quiet during home leave because he feared it would delay discharge. He now has tummy pain. His prescribed laxatives are senna and Laxido, and he takes hyoscine at night for drooling."},"task":"Assess his abdomen at the bedside in about ten minutes: focused red-flag questions, a systematic abdominal check, a NEWS2 score, and an urgent, safe plan.","first60":["Ask for a full set of observations and a NEWS2 score now; a low score does not rule out a bowel emergency.","Look at the drug chart: clozapine dose and timing, laxatives actually given, hyoscine or other anticholinergics, and when his last FBC was.","Check the bowel chart: when was the last entry, and who recorded it?","Bring a chaperone, gloves and a pillow; he must lie flat with one pillow.","Wash your hands and close the curtains."],"ask":["'Where is the pain, and how bad out of ten?'","'When did you last open your bowels, and are you passing wind?'","'Have you felt sick or been sick? What have you eaten today?'","'Any watery leakage, or blood in your underwear?'","'Which laxatives have you actually been taking, especially on leave?'","'Have you missed any clozapine doses?'","'Any operations on your tummy before?'"],"recognise":["Twelve days without a bowel action, no wind and new pain on clozapine: suspected bowel obstruction or ileus, a surgical emergency.","Clozapine slows the gut in most people who take it; hyoscine for drooling adds an anticholinergic hit.","No wind plus nausea and distension suggests the bowel has stopped working, not just simple constipation.","A low NEWS2 does not rule out danger: obstruction can perforate before the observations change.","Watery leakage can be overflow around impacted stool, not recovery.","Fear about discharge led him to hide symptoms: a reason to ask about bowels at every leave review."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the check, keep him nil by mouth, send bloods, hold tonight's clozapine pending senior and pharmacy advice, and arrange same-day surgical review at the acute hospital.","r":"Right: suspected clozapine-related bowel obstruction can be fatal and needs surgical assessment and imaging today.","best":true},{"c":"Give a phosphate enema and extra senna, and review tomorrow.","r":"Unsafe: stimulants and enemas before obstruction is excluded can worsen pain or cause perforation, and a day's delay could be fatal.","best":false},{"c":"Stop clozapine permanently because of the constipation.","r":"Not your decision alone, and not needed: most patients stay on clozapine with better bowel care. Holding doses needs a plan, since a gap over 48 hours means re-titration.","best":false}],"law":["He is informal and has capacity: he can agree to or refuse the check, the rectal check and the transfer. Explain the risks of refusing and record his decision.","Offer a chaperone for the abdominal and any rectal check, and record their name.","A serious harm that went unnoticed for 12 days needs an incident report on the local system."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Lewis, your bowel may have stopped moving because of the clozapine. That can be dangerous, so the surgeons at the main hospital need to see you today. Your discharge may move a little, but your safety comes first."},{"to":"Surgical registrar","s":"45-year-old man on clozapine 400 mg a day. No bowel action for 12 days, no flatus for two, nausea, colicky lower pain 7 out of 10. Distended, tympanic, tender lower abdomen, very quiet bowel sounds. Pulse 104. Query obstruction or ileus."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"Nil by mouth for now, please. Bloods are going. Hold his evening clozapine until I've spoken to the consultant and pharmacist, and stop the hyoscine. Call me at once if he vomits or the pain worsens."}],"write":"Urgent review, 11:50. Asked by NIC: no BO 12 days. Chaperone: SN Patel. Consent given.\nHx: colicky lower abdominal pain 2/7, now 7/10, constant ache. No flatus 2/7. Nausea, no vomiting. Not eaten today. No overflow, no PR blood. Stopped Laxido on leave (taste). No previous abdominal surgery. Clozapine 150 mg mane, 250 mg nocte, no missed doses. Hyoscine 300 mcg nocte.\nObs: HR 104, BP 118/76, RR 18, SpO2 98% RA, T 37.6, Alert. NEWS2 1.\nO/E: uncomfortable lying still. Dry mucous membranes. No jaundice or pallor. Abdomen distended, no scars, hernial orifices clear. Tender lower abdomen, worst LIF, on deep palpation. No guarding or rebound. No masses. Tympanic. No shifting dullness. Bowel sounds very quiet. PR offered, declined; capacity to decline.\nImp: suspected clozapine-induced GI hypomotility with obstruction or ileus.\nPlan: d/w surgical reg, accepted for same-day review. Ambulance booked, nurse escort. NBM. Bloods: FBC (incl neutrophils), U&E, CRP, LFT, amylase, lactate, clozapine level. Hold evening clozapine; d/w Dr Ahmed (consultant) and pharmacy re restart (>48 h gap = re-titrate). Stop hyoscine. No enema or stimulant laxative until obstruction excluded. Incident report done. Discharge on hold.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask about wind, vomiting and overflow: they separate obstruction from simple constipation.","Ask where it hurts before you touch, and press there last, watching his face.","Light then deep palpation of all nine regions, then percuss and listen for a full minute.","Offer the hernia and rectal checks with a chaperone, and record the answer.","Say the plan out loud: surgical review today, nil by mouth, bloods, clozapine held with a restart plan."],"next":"The surgeons see Lewis at 14:00. A CT shows a dilated colon full of faeces without perforation. He is treated conservatively and returns to the ward three days later. Clozapine restarts at 12.5 mg and is re-titrated with pharmacy. Laxido and senna are given daily under supervision, and discharge moves back by two weeks.","marks":["Hand hygiene, identity, consent, chaperone, and the pain located before starting.","Asked about last bowel action, wind, vomiting, overflow, laxatives, surgery and clozapine doses.","Systematic inspection, palpation, percussion and auscultation, tender area last.","Offered hernia and rectal checks with a chaperone.","Named suspected clozapine-related obstruction and arranged same-day surgical review.","Planned clozapine safely: held, consultant and pharmacist told, re-titration rule known."],"pitfalls":["Calling it constipation and prescribing an enema.","Being reassured by a normal NEWS2.","Forgetting that hyoscine for drooling worsens constipation.","Stopping clozapine for days with no restart plan, then restarting at the old dose.","Ignoring his discharge fear, so he hides symptoms again next time."],"refs":["1. MHRA Drug Safety Update: clozapine, reminder of potentially fatal risk of intestinal obstruction, faecal impaction and paralytic ileus (2017; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: clozapine, cautions and restarting after interruption (checked Nov 2026)","3. Clozaril summary of product characteristics (checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Physicians: NEWS2 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK psychiatric wards keep bowel charts for everyone on clozapine. Bowel problems on clozapine can be more deadly than its better-known blood problems, so a senior expects you to treat this as an emergency.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-18","n":1418,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"Dad's memory: a ten-minute collateral history from his daughter","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 10:00, new referrals clinic","where":"Older adult community mental health team base","text":"Tariq Hussain, a 68-year-old retired bus driver, has been referred by his GP with memory problems. He lives with his wife, Farida, who is well. He has agreed that you can talk to his daughter, Zainab Hussain, 34, who has come to the team base. Tariq is having his bloods taken next door."},"task":"Take a focused collateral history from Zainab in about ten minutes: the timeline, the thinking skills affected, daily living, other causes and the risks, then share your impression and next steps.","first60":["Read the GP letter: medicines, blood pressure, any stroke, diabetes or head injury, and recent bloods.","Check the consent: Tariq agreed to you speaking with Zainab. Say so at the start.","Have the order in your head: timeline, thinking skills, daily living, other causes, background, risks, summary.","Know the four common patterns: Alzheimer's, vascular, Lewy body and frontotemporal, plus depression, alcohol and medicines.","Plan to ask about driving, the cooker, money and the carer before you finish."],"ask":["'What first made you think something had changed, and when was that?'","'Has it come on gradually, or in sudden steps?'","'Does he struggle to find words, or call things by the wrong name?'","'Has he ever got lost somewhere he knows well?'","'Does he see things others can't, or swing between good and very muddled days?'","'Has his personality changed: ruder, more impulsive, less caring?'","'Is he still driving? Any near misses?'","'How is your mum coping?'"],"recognise":["A year of gradual, steady decline with early forgetting, word-finding trouble and getting lost: an Alzheimer's-type pattern.","Missed bills in a man who always handled them suggests his planning (executive function) is slipping too.","Sudden steps, strokes or many vascular risk factors suggest a vascular cause; check blood pressure and heart history.","Visual hallucinations, fluctuating alertness, falls or a tremor suggest Lewy body dementia, which changes drug safety.","Early personality change or disinhibition suggests frontotemporal dementia; low mood and poor effort suggest depression.","Getting lost while driving is a red-flag risk that needs advice today, not after the diagnosis."],"decide":[{"c":"Take a structured collateral history, screen for other causes and risks, advise that he stops driving until assessed, and explain the next tests.","r":"Right: it answers the GP's question, gathers what a memory clinic needs, and deals with today's risks.","best":true},{"c":"Tell Zainab it is probably Alzheimer's and that tablets will help.","r":"Premature: you have not screened other causes, and bloods, memory testing and a scan come before a diagnosis.","best":false},{"c":"Leave driving for after the diagnosis.","r":"Unsafe: he has already got lost while driving. Advise him not to drive until he has been assessed, and record that advice.","best":false}],"law":["Tariq consented to this conversation; record it. Share only what he would agree to, and ask before writing to family.","Driving: if dementia is diagnosed, he must tell the DVLA. If he keeps driving against advice, GMC guidance allows a doctor to inform the DVLA after telling him first.","Lasting power of attorney is best set up while he still has capacity to make it.","Financial scams against a vulnerable adult can be raised with adult social care as a safeguarding concern."],"say":[{"to":"Daughter","s":"From what you've described, a steady change over a year, starting with memory and words, fits a memory illness such as Alzheimer's disease. We need tests before we can say for sure."},{"to":"Daughter","s":"Because he got lost driving, I'll advise your dad not to drive until we've assessed him. I'll explain why to him myself today."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Collateral suggests 12 months of gradual memory, language and executive decline with no vascular, Lewy body or frontal features. Advised to stop driving pending assessment. Memory clinic work-up booked."}],"write":"Collateral history from daughter Zainab (34), with Tariq's consent. Older adult CMHT.\nOnset ~12 months: missed GP and dentist appointments; repeats questions. Got lost driving to mosque (familiar 30 yrs) last month. Word-finding difficulty. Steady decline, no steps, no fluctuation.\nFunction: beard untrimmed, shirt misbuttoned; missed bills, Zainab now manages. Cannot follow cricket commentary. Recognises family.\nNegatives: no visual hallucinations, falls, tremor or sleep acting-out; no personality change or disinhibition. Mood flat, not tearful. No alcohol. No head injury.\nPMH: HTN (amlodipine 5 mg). No stroke, DM or IHD. No known family dementia.\nRisks: still driving; burnt pan on hob x1; paid £300 to cold caller. Wife (Farida) exhausted.\nImp: gradual-onset cognitive decline, Alzheimer's type most likely; depression and vascular contribution to exclude.\nPlan: bloods (FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, B12, folate, HbA1c, calcium); ACE-III; MRI head. Advised no driving pending assessment (told Tariq). Herbert Protocol, bank trusted contact, carer's assessment for Farida. GP letter.\nDr Chinonso, ST4 old age psychiatry","make":["Start with her story, then structure: timeline before symptoms, symptoms before causes.","Ask for one dated example per domain: 'When did he last get lost? Where?'","Screen each differential with one or two sharp questions, not a long list.","Turn every risk into an action before she leaves.","Ask how the carer is coping: she is the person keeping him safe."],"next":"Tariq's bloods are normal. His ACE-III is 71/100, with memory and fluency most affected, and the MRI shows hippocampal atrophy. Alzheimer's disease is diagnosed at the memory clinic six weeks later. He stopped driving the day you saw him and has told the DVLA. Farida has a carer's assessment booked.","marks":["Confirmed consent, explained the purpose and invited her concerns first.","Established onset, duration and a gradual, steady course.","Asked about memory, word-finding, recognition, orientation and daily tasks.","Screened for vascular, Lewy body, frontotemporal, depressive, alcohol and medicine causes.","Explored driving, cooker, money and carer strain, with an action for each.","Summarised the likely cause and named the tests to come."],"pitfalls":["Diagnosing Alzheimer's before excluding other causes.","Forgetting medicines with anticholinergic effects, including over-the-counter sleep remedies.","Not asking about visual hallucinations and fluctuation, and missing Lewy body dementia.","Leaving driving until the diagnosis.","Never asking how his wife is coping."],"refs":["1. NICE NG97: dementia, assessment and management (checked Nov 2026)","2. DVLA: assessing fitness to drive, guide for medical professionals, dementia (checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC: confidentiality, patients' fitness to drive and reporting concerns to the DVLA (checked Nov 2026)","4. Alzheimer's Society: the Herbert Protocol (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK memory services, the family's account often carries more weight than a single memory test. Use one concrete example per area, and always ask about driving: the DVLA rules are strict and seniors will check you did.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-19","n":1419,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🌀","title":"Sudden confusion on the medical ward: a ten-minute collateral history from his son","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 15:30, liaison referral","where":"Acute medical ward, general hospital","text":"Gordon Whitfield, a 71-year-old widower, was admitted two days ago after a neighbour found him outside his house, half dressed and muttering. Since then he has been drowsy at times and has wandered into other bays. The ward consultant asks whether he is psychotic. He takes amlodipine and atorvastatin and has no known psychiatric history. He has agreed to you speaking with his son Rhys, 35, who is visiting and very worried."},"task":"Take a focused collateral history from Rhys in about ten minutes to clarify the cause of his father's change, explain your impression, and agree what happens next.","first60":["Read the medical notes: obs chart, NEWS2, any 4AT score, bloods, chest X-ray, fluid and bowel charts.","Check the drug chart for anything new: sedatives, opioids, anticholinergics, or antipsychotics given 'as required'.","Ask the nurse how he has been across the day: drowsy, agitated or both?","Find a quiet room; Rhys is upset and will want answers.","Have the key question ready: when did you last see him completely himself?"],"ask":["'When did you last see your dad completely himself?'","'Did the change come on over hours, days or months?'","'Is he better at some times of day than others?'","'Has he been seeing or hearing things that aren't there?'","'Had he been unwell before this: cough, fever, waterworks, a fall?'","'Before this, was he managing his own shopping, money and driving?'","'How has he been since your mum died?'"],"recognise":["Well a week ago, then acute confusion with drowsiness, poor attention, fluctuation and a reversed sleep pattern: delirium, not a new psychosis or dementia.","A cough with coloured phlegm the week before points to a chest infection as the likely cause.","Seeing things and muttering are common in delirium; fleeting, frightening misperceptions fit better than fixed delusions.","A fully independent baseline (driving, finances, minding grandchildren) argues against an established dementia, though delirium can unmask one.","Drowsy, quiet periods are hypoactive delirium: easy to miss and linked to worse outcomes.","Bereavement, self-neglect and living alone matter for recovery and discharge planning."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain that this looks like delirium, probably from a chest infection, advise the medical team on the delirium screen and non-drug care, and involve Rhys in reorientation.","r":"Right: it answers the referral question, treats the cause and uses the family as part of the treatment.","best":true},{"c":"Agree that he is psychotic and suggest regular olanzapine.","r":"Wrong: acute onset, fluctuation and inattention mean delirium. Regular antipsychotics add sedation, falls and stroke risk without treating the cause.","best":false},{"c":"Tell Rhys his father probably has dementia and should go into a care home.","r":"Premature and harmful: he was independent a week ago. Most delirium improves, and memory should be reassessed after recovery.","best":false}],"law":["Gordon agreed to you speaking with Rhys. If he lacks capacity for that decision later, you can still listen to family and share what is in his best interests.","If he tries to leave and lacks capacity to decide on his care, staff can use the Mental Capacity Act to act in his best interests. If restrictions continue, the ward applies for a Deprivation of Liberty Safeguards authorisation.","The Mental Health Act is rarely needed: delirium is treated as a physical illness."],"say":[{"to":"Son","s":"This looks like delirium: a sudden confusion caused by a physical illness, most likely the chest infection. It's common at your dad's age, and most people get better as the infection is treated."},{"to":"Son","s":"You can really help: visit at regular times, bring his glasses and some family photos, and calmly remind him where he is and why."},{"to":"Ward consultant","s":"Collateral confirms he was fully independent a week ago, with acute onset, fluctuating drowsiness and inattention after a productive cough. This is delirium, likely from a chest infection, not a primary psychosis."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. Collateral from son Rhys (35), with Gordon's consent.\nBaseline: well until ~1 week ago. Independent: drives, shops, manages finances online, minded grandchildren in park last week. No prior memory concerns. No psychiatric history.\nCurrent: found outside half dressed, muttering. On ward: drowsy at times, misnames Rhys (calls him by brother's name), found in another bay, sleeping by day, picking at air, not eating. Fluctuates; worse in evenings.\nPhysical: productive cough with green sputum the week before; Rhys advised GP. No known falls or head injury. Alcohol: 2 pints weekly. No new meds. Bowels unknown.\nBackground: widowed last year (wife, breast cancer). Since then ready meals, less care over appearance, quieter. No family dementia.\nRisks: wandering, falls, pulling cannula, lives alone, still drives.\nImp: delirium (mixed), likely secondary to LRTI. Not primary psychosis. Baseline argues against established dementia.\nAdvice to medical team: delirium screen incl CXR, cultures, U&E, calcium, glucose, bladder scan, bowel chart; treat cause; non-drug measures; avoid antipsychotics unless severely distressed or dangerous; falls bundle; no driving until GP review; recheck cognition and mood at 6-8 weeks.\nDr Chinonso, CT3 liaison psychiatry","make":["Anchor everything to one question: when was he last completely himself?","Ask about attention, fluctuation and drowsiness: the core of delirium.","Hunt for causes in order: infection, drugs, alcohol, falls, bowels, bladder, pain.","Get a detailed baseline: driving, money, shopping, new routes.","Answer the dementia question directly, then give Rhys a job."],"next":"Gordon's chest X-ray shows left basal pneumonia. With antibiotics, fluids, glasses back on and Rhys visiting each afternoon, he is lucid by day five. His 4AT is 0 at discharge. The GP rechecks his memory and mood at eight weeks; memory is normal, and he accepts bereavement support.","marks":["Confirmed consent, acknowledged Rhys's worry and explained the purpose.","Established last-seen-well, acute onset and fluctuation.","Asked about attention, orientation, sleep reversal, perceptual changes and eating.","Screened for infection, drugs, alcohol, falls, bowels and bladder.","Established a fully independent baseline with no prior memory decline.","Explained delirium versus dementia and gave Rhys a role."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'psychotic' without asking about onset and fluctuation.","Forgetting hypoactive delirium: drowsy, quiet and easy to miss.","Skipping bowels, bladder and medicines in the cause hunt.","Not asking about baseline function, so you cannot separate delirium from dementia.","Suggesting regular antipsychotics."],"refs":["1. NICE CG103: delirium, prevention, diagnosis and management (updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. 4AT rapid assessment test for delirium (checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Capacity Act 2005 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","4. DVLA: assessing fitness to drive (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: liaison teams in UK hospitals are often asked 'Is this psychosis?' when the answer is delirium. Your collateral history settles it. Write a clear, short note the medical team can act on.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-20","n":1420,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"💎","title":"Crystals and auras: diagnosing mania at 72 in A&E and assessing risk","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 16:20, liaison psychiatry","where":"Emergency department, general hospital","text":"Rosalind Hartley, a 72-year-old retired primary school teacher, came in with a split thumbnail and has upset staff by saying the doctor who treated her has a toxic aura. Her son Martin, 45, who lives with her, told you that for a month she has been absorbed in homeopathy and healing crystals, spends hours at the library, confronts visitors about cleansing their auras, shouts at him when he questions her, and has been ordering remedies online."},"task":"Take a focused history from Mrs Hartley in about ten minutes to reach a diagnosis, explain it to her, and assess risk.","first60":["Find a quiet side room away from the busy department; leave the door clear and tell the nurse where you are.","Read the A&E notes: obs, any head injury, current medicines including inhalers and recent steroids.","Have Martin's account fixed in your head so you can ask about specifics: library, spending, shouting.","Plan to let her talk first, then steer firmly: mood, beliefs, behaviour, physical causes, past, risk.","Expect to be interrupted. Keep questions short and closed when needed."],"ask":["'How many hours are you sleeping, and do you feel tired?'","'Do your thoughts feel faster than usual?'","'Do you feel you've been given special powers or a mission?'","'How much have you spent online this month?'","'Have you had any steroid tablets or new medicines recently?'","'Have you ever had a time like this before, or a spell of depression?'","'Has anyone in the family had problems with their moods?'","'Have you come close to hurting anyone, or yourself?'"],"recognise":["A month of reduced need for sleep, pressured speech, irritability, grandiosity, overspending and disinhibition: a manic episode.","Believing she can heal by touch and read energy is a grandiose, mood-congruent delusion: mania with psychotic features.","A spell of unexplained travel and spending, past depression and a father admitted for 'his moods' point to bipolar disorder.","A prednisolone course for her COPD before onset may have triggered or worsened the episode.","Mania first recognised in later life needs physical causes excluded: thyroid, calcium, infection, a brain lesion, dementia, medicines.","Risks: exhaustion and weight loss, debts, online exploitation, near-assault at the library, stopping her inhalers and swallowing a crystal."],"decide":[{"c":"Name a likely manic episode, explain it simply, assess all risks, and agree physical tests and a discussion with your consultant and Martin.","r":"Right: it answers the referral, takes physical causes seriously at 72 and keeps her safe while she decides.","best":true},{"c":"Conclude she has a new eccentric interest and discharge her home.","r":"Wrong: the sleep loss, speech, grandiosity, spending and aggression add up to mania, with real risks to her health and money.","best":false},{"c":"Diagnose late-onset dementia because she is 72.","r":"Premature: she is oriented with no memory complaints and has clear mood symptoms. Organic causes need tests, but the picture is mania.","best":false}],"law":["She is in A&E voluntarily. If she tries to leave and lacks capacity about treatment, short-term action under the Mental Capacity Act is possible; otherwise, request a Mental Health Act assessment through the AMHP service.","Section 5(2) holding powers cannot be used in A&E, because she is not an admitted inpatient.","With high risk and refusal of help, a Mental Health Act assessment is the lawful route.","Share information with Martin in her best interests where risk is high, and record why."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Mrs Hartley, I think your mood and energy have been running far too high for a month. Doctors call this mania. It's an illness, it isn't your fault, and it can be treated."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Before anything else, I'd like some blood tests and a scan, because at any age we need to check the body isn't causing this."},{"to":"Liaison consultant","s":"72-year-old woman with a month of manic symptoms and grandiose healing beliefs, after prednisolone for COPD. Probable past hypomania, family history. High financial and physical risks; she lacks insight. I think she needs admission."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, A&E. 72F retired teacher. Collateral from son Martin (45) earlier.\nHx: 1 month: sleeping ~3 h/night, not tired; racing thoughts; pressured speech; irritable; eating little, weight loss. Believes she can heal by touch and read 'auras'; plans to swallow a crystal to cure her COPD; stopped inhalers. No hallucinations. Spent ~£2,000 online; seller asked for bank details. Nearly struck librarian with collection tin. Shouts at Martin; no violence.\nPhysical: COPD; prednisolone 30 mg x 5 days ~5 weeks ago. No headache, head injury, vomiting or visual change. Oriented. No memory complaints. No alcohol or drugs.\nPast: depression after husband's death (50s), treated by GP. Unexplained 5-week trip to Spain with heavy spending in her 60s. FH: father admitted for 'moods'.\nMSE: pressured, distractible, elated and irritable, grandiose delusions, no insight. No suicidal ideation.\nImp: manic episode with psychotic features; probable bipolar affective disorder; steroids possible trigger; organic causes to exclude.\nRisk: high (self-neglect, COPD, crystal ingestion, finances, exploitation, aggression).\nPlan: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, glucose, CRP, B12, folate, syphilis serology, urine drug screen, ECG; CT head. Discuss with consultant; offer informal admission; if refuses, MHA assessment via AMHP. Inform Martin.\nDr Chinonso, CT3 liaison psychiatry","make":["Let her talk for a minute; then ask short, direct questions and summarise to steer.","Ask about sleep need, not just sleep: 'Do you feel tired?'","Ask about the past: a forgotten spell of overspending can be the clue to bipolar disorder.","Always ask about steroids and other medicines in new or late mania.","Turn each belief into a risk question: what will she do because of it?"],"next":"Her bloods and CT head are normal. Rosalind declines admission; an AMHP and two doctors assess her that evening and she is admitted under Section 2 to the older adult ward. She settles on olanzapine over three weeks, and lithium is discussed once she is well.","marks":["Built rapport with an irritable patient and let her talk first.","Established sleep, energy, racing thoughts, speech and irritability.","Explored grandiose beliefs and insight.","Asked about steroids, head injury, headaches, memory, thyroid and substances.","Asked about past depression, past highs and family history.","Assessed risk to self, others and from others, including money and her COPD."],"pitfalls":["Arguing with her beliefs instead of exploring them.","Forgetting steroids and other physical causes at 72.","Missing a past episode of overspending or travel, and so missing bipolar disorder.","Only asking about suicide and missing accidental, financial and exploitation risks.","Getting drawn into a long debate and running out of time to explain."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185: bipolar disorder, assessment and management (updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: prednisolone, psychiatric side effects (checked Nov 2026)","3. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","4. DVLA: psychiatric disorders and driving (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: older people with mania are often first seen in A&E or on medical wards, and the referral may say 'odd behaviour'. Seniors expect you to name mania, check steroids and the brain, and assess money and exploitation risk.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-21","n":1421,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"👃","title":"Poisoning neighbours: a focused history and mental state with an angry new admission","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 10:15, older adult ward","where":"Quiet lounge, older adult inpatient ward","text":"Mrs Maureen Cartwright, 68, a retired dinner lady who lives alone and has never married, came in overnight under Section 2 after police brought her in following months of calls and letters about her neighbours. The night nurse writes that she refused supper and shouted at staff. Your consultant asks you to see her this morning: a focused history and mental state before the ward round at 11."},"task":"In about ten minutes, take a focused history of her beliefs about the neighbours and record a targeted mental state: perceptions, mood, orientation, insight and risk.","first60":["Read the Section 2 papers and the police note: exactly what she wrote, said and did, and when the trouble began.","Check the night notes: did she eat, sleep, or say anything about food being poisoned on the ward?","Pick a room where she can see the door and you are not between her and it; ask a nurse to stay within call.","Set your order now: her story, the belief, perceptions, mood and cognition, risk, insight. Ten minutes, no full cognitive test today.","Note her appearance and behaviour from the doorway: the mental state starts before you speak."],"ask":["'In your words, how did you end up here last night?'","'When did you first notice something was wrong next door?'","'How sure are you that they mean to harm you, from 0 to 100?'","'Is there any other way the smell could be explained?'","'Do you ever notice smells, tastes or sounds that other people around you don't?'","'Have you had blackouts, funny turns, or odd feelings rising from your stomach?'","'Have you thought about protecting yourself, or keeping anything by you just in case?'","'What are you managing to eat at the moment, and where does it come from?'"],"recognise":["A single, organised, persecutory belief held for a year with linked parts (smell, buried cats, lost cat, plot to poison her): a systematised delusion.","Clear thinking, intact orientation and no negative symptoms point towards delusional disorder; keep late-onset schizophrenia-like psychosis, psychotic depression and dementia on the list.","The rotten-egg smell may be an olfactory hallucination, but a real gas or drain problem must be excluded before you write that.","Olfactory hallucinations should prompt questions about seizures: temporal lobe epilepsy or a structural lesion are treatable causes.","Risk is mostly to herself: eating little for fear of poison, poor sleep, isolation and escalating conflict. Violence is unlikely but must still be asked about.","No insight: she sees herself as a victim, not unwell. Record her own words."],"decide":[{"c":"Validate her fear, keep asking the key questions calmly, step back when 'I'm not mad' flares and return through easier topics, then record a targeted MSE and request an organic screen and a home gas check.","r":"Right: you get the belief, perceptions, mood, risk and insight without a fight, and you test the real-world explanation as well as the medical ones.","best":true},{"c":"Point out, kindly but firmly, that a missing cat and a smell cannot prove a poisoning plot.","r":"Wrong: arguing with a delusion hardens it and ends the conversation. Your job today is to describe it, not dismantle it.","best":false},{"c":"Stop when she stands up and shouts, and write 'too hostile to assess'.","r":"Wrong: you already have most of what you need, and her anger is itself a finding. Let her pace, validate, and finish with gentler questions.","best":false}],"law":["Section 2 MHA: detention for assessment for up to 28 days. Check the papers are in order and that her rights (s132) have been explained aloud and in writing.","She can apply to the Tribunal within the first 14 days and can have an Independent Mental Health Advocate: tell her both today and record it.","Medication for mental disorder can be given without consent in the first three months (s63), but seek her agreement first and record her views.","Sharing information with police or the council needs her consent unless there is a clear risk to others."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I can see you're angry about being brought here, and frightened about what's been happening at home. I'm not here to argue. I want to hear it from you."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"Fixed persecutory belief about the neighbours with a possible smell hallucination. She's oriented and denies harming anyone, but she's barely eating. Please chart food, fluids and sleep, and offer sealed packaged food."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Mrs Cartwright, 68: a year-long systematised persecutory delusion with a probable olfactory hallucination, no insight, mood not depressed, oriented. Main risk is self-neglect. I've requested an organic screen and asked for a gas safety check at her home."}],"write":"Ward review, Day 1, Section 2. 68F, retired dinner lady, lives alone. Brought by police after months of calls and letters about neighbours.\nHx: ~1 year believes neighbours buried cats in garden (explains rotten-egg smell in her house), took her cat last year, now plan to poison her because she 'knows too much'. Letters to neighbours and council, repeated police calls. Eats only packaged food from another town; sleeps poorly for fear of poison. No funny turns or blackouts. No alcohol or drugs. Wears reading glasses; hearing adequate in conversation.\nMSE: tidy, irritable, guarded; stood and paced when asked about voices, settled with reassurance. Speech normal rate, coherent. Mood 'fine'; not objectively depressed. Thought: fixed, systematised persecutory delusion, conviction 100%. Perception: probable olfactory hallucination (rotten eggs); denies voices, visions, taste or touch changes. Cognition: oriented to time, place and person; no formal testing today. Insight: none; sees herself as a victim of injustice.\nRisk: to self: poor intake, isolation, vulnerability (moderate). To others: denies plans to confront neighbours, no weapons (low). No self-harm thoughts.\nImpression: likely delusional disorder; ddx late-onset schizophrenia-like psychosis, psychotic depression, dementia, temporal lobe epilepsy.\nPlan: bloods (FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, B12, folate, glucose), ECG, CT head; food and fluid chart; sealed food offered; ask care coordinator to arrange gas safety check; rights and IMHA re-explained; discuss at ward round.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Sit down early, at an angle, and let her finish her first rant: two minutes of listening buys you the next eight.","Ask about the belief with curiosity, not challenge: 'What made you sure?' gets more than 'Why do you think that?'","Screen every sense, and name smell and taste first: that is where this story points.","When a question lands badly, apologise once, move to something safer, and come back to it later.","Turn every observation into a written MSE line: appearance, behaviour, speech, mood, thought, perception, cognition, insight.","Finish with what happens next in plain words, even if she doesn't accept it."],"next":"Her bloods and CT head are normal. The care coordinator arranges a gas safety check: no leak is found. Over two weeks, with one regular nurse and sealed meals, she starts eating. She agrees to try amisulpride 50 mg to 'help with the nerves', and the fear eases although the belief remains.","marks":["Acknowledged her anger early and kept a calm, non-confronting manner throughout.","Established the content, onset, evidence and conviction of the persecutory belief.","Screened all five senses and asked about seizures because of the smell.","Covered mood, sleep, eating and orientation briefly.","Asked directly about risk to neighbours, to herself and from self-neglect.","Recorded absent insight and summarised a targeted mental state."],"pitfalls":["Arguing with the belief, or agreeing with it to keep the peace.","Abandoning perceptions after 'I'm not mad' and never asking about smell or taste.","Calling the smell a hallucination without asking whether anyone has checked for gas.","Taking a full life history and running out of time for risk and insight.","Forgetting she may not be eating because she fears poison."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178: psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapters on Section 2 and patients' rights (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG97: dementia assessment, for the organic screen (2018; checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: amisulpride, older adults (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, a new Section 2 patient will be asked about everything again and again. Name that you know she has told her story before, keep it short, and say exactly what will happen next.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-47","n":1447,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🏠","title":"Ready for discharge, but he can't go home: planning where next","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 11:00, ward round follow-up","where":"Older adults psychiatric ward, quiet room","text":"Bernard Kowalski, 74, was bailed to the ward after assaulting his wife. His mood and sleep are better on mirtazapine then trazodone, CT head showed only age-related atrophy, and he no longer needs to be in hospital. Yesterday his wife Halina and son Tomasz told the ward about years of heavy drinking and violence; Halina has an injunction stopping him coming home. Bernard does not know yet. The consultant asks you to tell him, work out what help he needs and start the discharge plan. History and risk assessment are already done."},"task":"In about ten minutes, tell Bernard he cannot return home, hear his reaction, establish what he can do for himself day to day and with money, explore where he would like to live, and agree realistic discharge options with your reasons.","first60":["Check the notes for the exact injunction terms the family gave, and his bail conditions and officer in the case.","Read the OT and nursing notes: what has he done for himself on the ward (washing, dressing, tablets, the ward kitchen)?","Know his medication: mirtazapine or trazodone, current dose, and whether he can name them.","Ask the ward social worker if a Care Act referral or housing referral has been started.","Plan your warning shot and the one sentence that tells him; do not drift into a history or a risk assessment."],"ask":["'What's your understanding of why you came into hospital, and what's happened since?'","'You seem unsure about some recent events. Have you noticed problems with your memory?'","'What are your thoughts on what I've just told you?'","'How have things been between you and Halina lately?'","'Who did the cooking, washing and shopping at home? What can you do yourself?'","'Who sorted out the bills and your pension? Do you know what comes in each month?'","'If home isn't possible right now, where would you like to be? What worries you most?'","'What do you think of the options I've described?'"],"recognise":["Vague about recent events and the tests he has had: note possible memory problems, and plan cognitive testing once settled, but do not test now.","He washes and dresses himself, but Halina did all the cooking, tablets, shopping and bills: he will need substantial practical help.","Denial or minimising ('one argument in forty-eight years') is common when bad news lands; acknowledge it rather than debate it.","A belief that his wife is unfaithful, if he raises it, is important for the team and for future safety: note it and pass it on, but this conversation is not the risk assessment.","He is bailed to hospital: discharge to any address must be coordinated with the police and his solicitor so bail conditions are varied first.","He will be homeless on discharge without a plan: the hospital has a legal duty to refer him to the local housing authority, with his consent."],"decide":[{"c":"Break the news clearly, hear him, map daily-living and money skills, explore his wishes, then offer sheltered or extra-care housing with a care package or residential care, each with benefits and barriers; start Care Act, OT, housing and bail actions.","r":"Right: honest, practical and shared, with every next step owned by a named person.","best":true},{"c":"Avoid mentioning the injunction and say the social worker will sort out where he goes.","r":"Wrong: he has a right to know why he cannot go home, and he cannot take part in planning without it.","best":false},{"c":"Discharge him to a flat of his own with no support because he can wash and dress.","r":"Unsafe: he has never cooked, managed tablets or paid bills, may have memory problems and is drinking. That plan is likely to fail quickly.","best":false}],"law":["He is an informal patient with bail conditions: liaise with the officer in the case and his solicitor before discharge so a new bail address is agreed.","The injunction is a court order: he must not return home or contact his wife except as the order allows. Breach is a criminal matter.","Care Act 2014: the local authority must assess his care and support needs; the ward social worker makes the referral.","Homelessness Reduction Act 2017: the trust must refer him to the local housing authority, with his consent, as he is at risk of homelessness.","He is presumed to have capacity for decisions about where to live; if memory problems raise doubt, assess capacity for that specific decision under the MCA 2005."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm afraid I have some difficult news. Halina has got a court order which means you can't go back to the house. Tomasz and the family support her decision. I'm sorry; this must be a real shock."},{"to":"Patient","s":"From what you've told me, Halina did a lot for you. I think you'll need some help each day after you leave, and I want to find somewhere that gives you that."},{"to":"Ward social worker","s":"Mr Kowalski now knows he can't go home. He manages personal care but has never cooked, managed tablets or money. Can you start a Care Act assessment and a housing referral? He prefers sheltered housing over a care home."}],"write":"Discharge planning discussion. 74M, informal, bailed to ward. Mood improved on current antidepressant. CT: age-related atrophy only.\nInformed: wife has injunction preventing return home; family supportive of this. Reaction: shocked, initially denied violence; acknowledged drinking 'most days'. Raised belief wife may have 'someone else' - noted, not explored further today; for MDT and consultant.\nUnderstanding of admission: vague about recent events and tests - possible memory difficulty; for cognitive assessment when settled.\nFunction: independent washing, dressing, mobility. Never cooked, never managed tablets, shopping or bills (wife did). Unsure of pension amount.\nWishes: would prefer 'own place with someone popping in'; fears care home and being alone.\nOptions discussed with pros/cons: sheltered/extra-care housing with care package (preferred); residential care if needs higher; own flat without support not advised.\nPlan: ward SW - Care Act assessment + Duty to Refer to housing (consent given); OT - kitchen and medication assessment; pharmacy - blister pack/compliance aid; police OIC and solicitor re bail address; alcohol liaison; ACE-III when settled; discharge planning meeting in 1 week. Consultant informed.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Say the news early and plainly, then stop and let him react.","Ask about tasks one by one: washing, dressing, tablets, cooking, shopping, bills.","Name the conclusion out loud: 'You'll need help with X and Y.'","Offer two or three options with one benefit and one barrier each, not a single decision.","Ask what he thinks, and record his preference.","End with who does what next, by when."],"next":"The ward social worker completes the Care Act assessment and housing referral. The OT kitchen assessment confirms he needs prepared meals and a compliance aid. His solicitor agrees a new bail address, and two weeks later he moves to an extra-care flat with a morning and evening care call, alcohol team follow-up and a memory clinic appointment.","marks":["Checked his understanding of the admission and noted memory difficulty.","Broke the news of the injunction clearly and with a warning shot.","Elicited his response to the family's account, including drinking and feelings about his wife.","Assessed daily living tasks and money management in detail.","Elicited his wishes and worries about where to live.","Offered realistic options with benefits and barriers and named next steps."],"pitfalls":["Taking a full history or launching a risk assessment when the task excludes both.","Avoiding or softening the injunction until it is unclear.","Assuming he can manage because he washes and dresses himself.","Offering a care home as the only option, or none at all.","Forgetting the bail conditions and the housing referral."],"refs":["1. Care Act 2014 and statutory guidance: needs assessment (checked Nov 2026)","2. Homelessness Reduction Act 2017: duty to refer, public authorities including hospitals (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG27: transition between inpatient hospital settings and community or care home settings (checked Nov 2026)","4. Mental Capacity Act 2005 Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK hospitals, social care and housing are arranged by the local authority, not the NHS. Your job is to describe needs precisely, refer early and keep the patient's wishes at the centre.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-51","n":1451,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"💧","title":"Seclusion review: not drinking for days","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 15:40, day shift","where":"Psychiatric intensive care unit (PICU), seclusion suite","text":"Marcus Ferreira, 27, has paranoid schizophrenia and stopped his clozapine about a week before admission. He came to the PICU three days ago and has been in seclusion since he hit a nurse on day one. He refuses medication and observations and has drunk very little. Today he has a headache and a dry mouth. Sean, the nurse covering seclusion, bleeps you to review him."},"task":"Take a focused handover from the seclusion nurse, recognise likely dehydration in a man who will not be examined, and agree a realistic, staffed plan for observations, bloods, fluids, medication and possible transfer to A&E.","first60":["Bring the seclusion record, the drug chart and the section papers: you need legal status, last medical review time and what has been offered.","Ask for the fluid chart before you reach the door: millilitres per day, not 'not much'.","Look through the vision panel first: colour, posture, breathing, alertness. Do not open the door alone.","Check who on shift is trained in restraint and who holds the bleep today.","Know your fallback drug before you start: IM lorazepam, because nobody has an ECG and he is likely dry."],"ask":["'What's worrying you most about Marcus right now?'","'How much has he actually drunk each day, in millilitres?'","'When did he last pass urine?'","'Has he seemed muddled, dizzy or sleepier than before?'","'Has anyone managed any observations, bloods or an ECG this admission?'","'What is he frightened of when he refuses food and drink?'","'Is there anything he will accept from us, such as a sealed bottle?'","'How many restraint-trained staff can we get here in the next half hour?'"],"recognise":["About 600 mL, then 300 mL, then only sips over three days, with no urine for 12 hours: he is very likely dehydrated, and possibly developing acute kidney injury.","Headache, dry mouth, dizziness, sunken eyes or new muddle are warning signs; confusion means he is getting worse.","No observations, bloods or ECG for three days in a man who was restrained: you have no physical baseline at all.","His refusal is driven by paranoia (fear that staff want his DNA or have tampered with food), not simple choice.","Dehydration, restraint, agitation and antipsychotics together raise the risk of neuroleptic malignant syndrome and rhabdomyolysis.","Short staffing makes any hands-on plan unsafe until more trained staff arrive."],"decide":[{"c":"Offer sealed drinks now; bring in enough trained staff; then do observations, bloods and ECG, using IM lorazepam if he cannot be assessed safely; transfer to A&E if he is clinically dry or the bloods are abnormal.","r":"Right: starts with the least restrictive step, plans for it failing, chooses the safer drug for a dehydrated man and sets clear triggers for transfer.","best":true},{"c":"Wait another day and keep offering drinks through the hatch.","r":"Wrong: three days of poor intake and no urine for 12 hours is already a medical problem. Waiting risks kidney injury and collapse.","best":false},{"c":"Give IM haloperidol and promethazine now so the team can get in.","r":"Unsafe here: no ECG, likely dehydration and recent restraint all raise the risk of arrhythmia and neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Use lorazepam first.","best":false}],"law":["Detained under Section 3. Medication for his mental disorder, including rapid tranquillisation, can be given without consent under Section 63 in the first three months.","Seclusion follows the MHA Code of Practice: last resort, shortest time, medical reviews on a fixed schedule and a written record of each.","If he refuses fluids or blood tests for a physical problem, assess his capacity for that decision. Where the refusal flows from his psychosis, ask the RC whether Section 63 covers it; otherwise use MCA best interests.","Escorted transfer to A&E needs Section 17 leave from his RC; record it.","Mental Health Units (Use of Force) Act 2018: record every use of force, avoid prone holds and use the least restrictive option."],"say":[{"to":"Sean, seclusion nurse","s":"You were right to call. Three days of very little fluid and no urine since this morning makes this a medical problem first. Let's plan how we get in safely, not just whether."},{"to":"Bleep holder","s":"I need three more restraint-trained staff on PICU within 30 minutes for a planned physical check on a secluded patient who is likely dehydrated. Can you send them from the other wards?"},{"to":"Consultant (SBAR)","s":"Marcus Ferreira, day 3 seclusion, about 900 mL in three days, no urine for 12 hours, headache and dry mouth, no obs or bloods. Plan: sealed fluids, planned obs and bloods with lorazepam if needed, A&E if abnormal. Are you happy?"}],"write":"Medical seclusion review, PICU, 15:50. Marcus Ferreira, 27M, Section 3, paranoid schizophrenia, clozapine stopped about 10 days ago. Seclusion since day 1 after assault on staff.\nHandover (Sean, RMN): intake about 600 mL day 1, 300 mL day 2, sips from tap since; no urine 12 h. Headache, dry mouth today; seen lying down more, cracked lips. No obs, bloods or ECG this admission (refused). Threatening, refuses unsealed food and all meds; believes staff want his DNA.\nImpression: probable dehydration, risk of AKI, rhabdomyolysis and NMS. Seclusion still needed for risk to others.\nPlan: 1) Sealed bottled water and oral rehydration offered now, opened in view. 2) Bleep holder to send 3 trained staff by 16:30. 3) Planned obs (NEWS2), capillary glucose, bloods (U&E, CK, FBC, LFT, glucose, CRP) and ECG. 4) If unable safely: IM lorazepam 1-2 mg; no IM antipsychotic until ECG and CK known; obs every 15 min for 1 h. 5) A&E with escorts and Section 17 leave if NEWS2 5+, confusion, AKI or high CK. 6) Clozapine: retitrate from 12.5 mg when accepting oral meds; pharmacy informed.\nConsultant informed 15:55, agrees. Next medical review 19:50. Fluid chart started.\nDr Chinonso, CT1","make":["Turn 'not drinking much' into numbers: millilitres per day and hours since urine.","Name the gap: no observations, bloods or ECG. Then name the likely problem: dehydration.","Never rely on persuasion alone: say what you will do if he still refuses.","Choose lorazepam for rapid tranquillisation and say why: dehydration, no ECG, risk of NMS.","Set objective triggers for A&E and explain how the transfer will be staffed.","Agree times: when the staff arrive, when you return, when the consultant is told."],"next":"Marcus takes two sealed bottles of water when Sean opens them in front of him, but refuses observations. With five trained staff he accepts IM lorazepam 2 mg. His pulse is 118, urea and creatinine are raised and CK is 2,400. He is transferred to A&E with two escorts for IV fluids and returns to the PICU two days later.","marks":["Took a structured handover covering admission, legal status and why seclusion began.","Quantified fluid intake and urine output over three days.","Asked for symptoms and signs of dehydration and noted the absent observations and bloods.","Planned sealed fluids first, then staffed observations, bloods and ECG.","Chose IM lorazepam and explained the NMS risk with antipsychotics.","Set clear A&E triggers, escorts and Section 17 leave, informed the consultant and documented."],"pitfalls":["Relying only on persuasion, with no plan for when he refuses.","Giving IM haloperidol to a dehydrated man with no ECG.","Never putting a number on fluid intake or urine output.","Planning restraint with too few trained staff.","Sending him to A&E without escorts, Section 17 leave or a medical handover."],"refs":["1. NICE NG10: violence and aggression, short-term management (2015; checked Nov 2026)","2. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015), chapter 26: seclusion (checked Nov 2026)","3. Royal College of Physicians: NEWS2 (2017; checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: lorazepam injection; clozapine, restarting after a break (checked Nov 2026)","5. Mental Health Units (Use of Force) Act 2018, statutory guidance (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK units, seclusion reviews are legal duties with set times, and nurses expect the doctor to lead a realistic plan. 'Keep encouraging fluids' is not a plan. Give numbers, a drug, staffing and a time you will return.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-55","n":1455,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🧭","title":"Memory loss and wandering: wife's account","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:00, memory clinic","where":"Memory assessment service, clinic room 2","text":"Leonard Harper, 79, a retired electrician, has had worsening memory for about a year. His GP has referred him, and he is having his own assessment with a colleague next door. His wife, Gwen, is very worried about his safety: he has recently wandered out of the house and been found lost. Leonard has agreed for you to talk to her."},"task":"Take a focused collateral history from Mrs Harper to form an initial impression, identify the safety risks, and explain what happens next.","first60":["Confirm Leonard's consent to speak to Gwen is recorded on the referral or by your colleague.","Read the GP letter: which bloods are done (B12, folate, TFT, U&E, calcium, glucose, FBC) and the full medicines list.","Scan the medicines for anticholinergics (bladder tablets, tricyclics, antihistamines).","Have the clinic's contact number and a carers' information leaflet ready to give her.","Plan your time: history first, then at least two minutes to explain next steps."],"ask":["'When did you first notice a change in Leonard?'","'Did it come on gradually, or in sudden steps?'","'Is it more recent things he forgets, or things from long ago?'","'Does he struggle to find words, use gadgets, or recognise people?'","'Talk me through the times he went missing. What time of day, and how was he found?'","'Has anything happened with cooking, the gas, driving or phone calls?'","'Has he seen things that aren't there, or had falls or shaking?'","'What tablets is he on? Any for his bladder?'","'How is all this affecting you?'"],"recognise":["A gradual decline over a year, repeating questions and losing recent memories while older ones stay clear: the typical pattern of Alzheimer's disease.","Word-finding problems, trouble with the remote and handing over the bills show spread beyond memory, with loss of everyday function: consistent with dementia, not just mild impairment.","No stepwise change or stroke, but high blood pressure: mixed Alzheimer's and vascular disease is possible.","No hallucinations, falls, tremor or personality change: Lewy body and frontotemporal dementias are less likely.","Oxybutynin is strongly anticholinergic and can worsen memory: flag it for review.","Wandering at night, a pan left on the gas, still driving and a near-scam are urgent safety risks; Gwen's exhaustion is a risk too."],"decide":[{"c":"Tell her it sounds like a memory illness, possibly Alzheimer's, explain the tests and diagnosis appointment, and agree safety steps now: Herbert Protocol, no driving until assessed, gas safety, telecare, and a carer's assessment.","r":"Right: honest about the likely diagnosis without jumping ahead, and acts on today's risks rather than waiting for the result.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her it is normal ageing and to come back if it worsens.","r":"Wrong: getting lost, losing everyday skills and a year of decline are not normal ageing, and the risks need action now.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her he definitely has Alzheimer's and start donepezil today.","r":"Premature: the diagnosis needs his own cognitive assessment, bloods and usually a scan. Medication follows a confirmed diagnosis.","best":false}],"law":["Leonard has agreed to you speaking to Gwen; you can listen to her concerns freely, and share his results with her if he agrees.","DVLA: a person diagnosed with dementia must tell the DVLA. Advise him to stop driving until the assessment is complete.","Lasting power of attorney (health and welfare, property and finance) can be made only while he has capacity: raise it early.","Locking him in the house to stop wandering creates a fire hazard and limits his liberty: discuss safer options."],"say":[{"to":"Gwen","s":"From what you've told me, it sounds like a memory illness, possibly Alzheimer's disease. We can't be sure until we've finished his tests, but there's a clear plan, and support for you both."},{"to":"Gwen","s":"You've been carrying a lot on your own. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment from adult social care, and I'd like to help you ask for one."},{"to":"Colleague assessing Leonard","s":"Collateral from his wife: gradual decline over a year, recent memory loss, word-finding problems, getting lost twice at night, pan left on the gas, still driving. He's on oxybutynin. No hallucinations, falls or stepwise change."}],"write":"Memory clinic, collateral history from wife (Gwen Harper), with Leonard's consent.\nLeonard Harper, 79M, retired electrician. About 1 year gradual decline: repeats questions, forgets conversations, loses keys daily; distant memories intact. Word-finding difficulty; cannot use TV remote or new phone; Gwen took over finances 6 months ago. Recognises family.\nRisks: lost twice in the last month (walked towards old depot, once at 10 pm; brought home by neighbours). Left pan on gas hob once. Still drives short trips; recent gatepost scrape. Near phone scam. No falls.\nBehaviour: quieter, gave up allotment; irritable when corrected. No hallucinations, REM sleep behaviour, tremor or disinhibition. Mood: not obviously low.\nPMH: hypertension (amlodipine), no stroke/TIA. Oxybutynin for bladder, about 18 months. Alcohol 2 pints weekly. Hearing aid. Family: sister had dementia in her 80s.\nADLs: washes and dresses with prompting; continent.\nCarer: Gwen tired, tearful, sleeping poorly; daughter nearby.\nImpression: probable dementia, likely Alzheimer's (possible vascular contribution). Anticholinergic burden.\nPlan: Leonard's cognitive assessment today; check GP bloods; CT head; GP to review oxybutynin; advised no driving pending assessment; Herbert Protocol, telecare (door sensor, GPS) and gas isolation valve via adult social care referral; carer's assessment; Alzheimer's Society; LPA discussed; diagnosis appointment about 4 weeks; clinic number given.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 old age psychiatry","make":["Pin down onset and course: gradual or stepwise, recent or remote memory.","Go beyond memory: language, recognition, gadgets, money.","Ask about each safety risk: wandering, gas, driving, scams, falls.","Screen for Lewy body and frontotemporal features and vascular risks.","Check the medicines list for anticholinergics.","Ask about her, then explain next steps in plain words with a contact number."],"next":"Leonard's cognitive testing shows marked recent-memory loss, and his CT scan shows generalised atrophy with mild small-vessel change. The GP stops oxybutynin. At the diagnosis appointment, Alzheimer's disease is confirmed and donepezil is started. A telecare door sensor is fitted, the Herbert Protocol form is filed, he stops driving, and Gwen joins a carers' group.","marks":["Established gradual onset over a year with recent memory loss.","Screened other cognitive areas and everyday function.","Identified wandering, gas, driving and scam risks.","Screened for features of other dementias, vascular risk and anticholinergic medicines.","Asked about Gwen's own wellbeing and support.","Gave a likely impression and clear next steps, with immediate safety advice."],"pitfalls":["Asking only about memory and missing function and safety.","Missing the driving and gas risks.","Not noticing an anticholinergic drug on the list.","Ignoring the carer's strain.","Giving a definite diagnosis before his assessment, or no impression at all."],"refs":["1. NICE NG97: dementia, assessment and management (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. DVLA: dementia and driving (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: oxybutynin; donepezil (checked Nov 2026)","4. Alzheimer's Society: dementia advisers, Herbert Protocol (checked Nov 2026)","5. Care Act 2014: carer's assessment (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK memory services rely heavily on the informant. Expect to be asked about driving, the Herbert Protocol, lasting power of attorney and carers' assessments in the first appointment, not only at diagnosis.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-u-64","n":1464,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🦋","title":"Thyroid check on lithium","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 14:00, psychiatry outpatients","where":"Outpatient clinic room with a couch","text":"Gareth Pugh, 52, has bipolar disorder and takes lithium carbonate 1000 mg at night. His GP has written: recent bloods show low T3 and T4 with a raised TSH. He is in clinic today. You need to ask about symptoms of thyroid problems, explain the results, and examine his thyroid and related signs. His bipolar history is not needed today."},"task":"In about ten minutes, ask about thyroid symptoms, explain the results in plain words, examine the thyroid and related signs, and tell him what happens next.","first60":["Read the GP letter: exact TSH and free T4, date, and the last lithium level, eGFR and calcium.","Check his other medicines: NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors or diuretics change lithium levels.","Book a chaperone, have a glass of water, a tendon hammer and a stethoscope ready.","Plan: two minutes of symptoms, one minute explaining, five minutes examining, one to two minutes closing.","Do not drift into his bipolar history; that is not today's job."],"ask":["'Have you been more tired than usual?'","'Has your weight changed? How is your appetite?'","'Do you feel the cold more than other people?'","'Any change in your skin, hair, bowels or voice?'","'Any swelling in your neck or trouble swallowing?'","'How has your mood and energy been? Any felt that life isn't worth living?'","'Does anyone in your family have thyroid problems?'","'Are you taking any other medicines, including painkillers from the chemist?'"],"recognise":["High TSH with low T4 is primary hypothyroidism: the pituitary is shouting at a gland that is not keeping up.","Lithium blocks thyroid hormone release and commonly causes hypothyroidism and goitre, especially in women and those with thyroid antibodies.","Low mood, tiredness, feeling the cold and putting on weight overlap with bipolar depression: treat the thyroid before changing mood medicines.","Signs to find: slow pulse, dry skin, puffy face, smooth goitre, slow-relaxing ankle reflexes.","A hard, irregular or rapidly growing lump, stridor or hoarseness needs an urgent two-week-wait referral, not routine follow-up.","Hypothyroidism does not usually mean stopping lithium; levothyroxine is added and lithium continues."],"decide":[{"c":"Ask focused symptoms, explain the results, examine hands, pulse, neck (with swallow and palpation from behind), eyes and reflexes, then continue lithium and recommend levothyroxine with repeat bloods.","r":"Right: confirms clinical hypothyroidism, keeps the mood stabiliser that keeps him well, and treats the cause.","best":true},{"c":"Stop lithium today because it has damaged his thyroid.","r":"Wrong: lithium-induced hypothyroidism is easily treated. Stopping lithium suddenly risks relapse, especially mania, and is a consultant decision.","best":false},{"c":"Explain the results and send him back to the GP without examining.","r":"Wrong: the job includes examining him. A goitre, nodule or slow pulse changes the plan and the urgency.","best":false}],"law":["Verbal consent for each part of the physical check; offer a chaperone and record whether one was present.","NICE CG185: on lithium, check thyroid function, renal function and calcium every 6 months, more often if abnormal.","Shared care: write to the GP the same day with findings and recommendations, and update his lithium record book.","Do not stop or change lithium without the consultant's agreement."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Your thyroid is a gland in the neck that sets your body's speed. The test shows it's running slow, and your brain is pushing it harder. Lithium commonly does this."},{"to":"Patient","s":"The good news: it's very treatable with a daily thyroid tablet, and you can usually stay on lithium."},{"to":"GP","s":"Mr Pugh has symptomatic lithium-associated hypothyroidism with a small smooth goitre. Please consider starting levothyroxine. Lithium continues. We've sent lithium level, U&E, calcium and TPO antibodies."}],"write":"Psychiatry OPD. Gareth Pugh, 52M, BPAD on lithium carbonate 1000 mg nocte. GP letter: low T3/T4, raised TSH.\nSx: 4 months tiredness, 5 kg gain, feels the cold, dry skin, constipated, low energy. No neck swelling, dysphagia or voice change. FHx: mother on thyroxine. Occasional ibuprofen for knee (advised paracetamol). Mood flat; no felt that life isn't worth living.\nO/E (chaperone: HCA present): dry skin, coarse hair, mild facial puffiness. P 56 reg. No tremor. Neck: small smooth diffuse goitre, moves on swallowing, not with tongue protrusion; no nodules, no lymphadenopathy, trachea central, no bruit. Eyes: no lid retraction, lid lag or proptosis. Ankle reflexes slow-relaxing bilaterally. No pretibial myxoedema.\nImp: primary hypothyroidism, likely lithium-induced.\nExplained results; lithium to continue.\nPlan: lithium level (12 h), U&E, eGFR, calcium, TPO antibodies today; ECG today (bradycardia). GP letter recommending levothyroxine and TSH recheck every 3 months until stable. Lithium book updated. Safety net: toxicity signs; urgent review if neck swelling grows or swallowing becomes hard.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 psychiatry","make":["Keep symptoms to two minutes: tired, weight, cold, skin, bowels, mood, neck.","Explain TSH as the brain's signal before using the word.","Inspect the neck from the front with a sip of water and with the tongue out.","Palpate from behind and ask him to swallow again.","Finish with ankle reflexes: slow relaxation is the sign.","Tell him the findings and that lithium usually continues."],"next":"The GP starts levothyroxine. Three months later his TSH is in range, he has lost 3 kg, and he says the fog has lifted. His lithium level is 0.7 mmol/L and lithium continues unchanged. TFTs, U&E and calcium go on to 6-monthly checks.","marks":["Asked the key hypothyroid symptoms and neck symptoms without a bipolar history.","Explained the results and the lithium link in plain words.","Examined hands, pulse and skin before the neck.","Inspected and palpated the neck correctly with swallowing.","Checked eyes and ankle reflexes.","Gave findings, a clear plan and a toxicity safety net."],"pitfalls":["Spending five minutes on symptoms and never reaching the reflexes.","Saying 'raised TSH' without explaining it.","Telling him to stop lithium.","Palpating from the front only, without asking him to swallow.","Taking a bipolar history when told not to."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185: bipolar disorder, lithium monitoring (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG145: thyroid disease, assessment and management (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: lithium carbonate; levothyroxine (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS: underactive thyroid (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, lithium is shared between psychiatry and the GP. Your letter is the plan: name the test, the drug recommendation and who acts on it.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"}
);
